rynas for Debian
================

Services
--------
- rynasd.service / rynasd.socket: the privileged daemon (root). It checks
  passwords with PAM (service /etc/pam.d/rynas) and keeps TOTP secrets in
  /var/lib/rynas/auth (0700 root). It listens only on /run/rynas/rynasd.sock
  (0660 root:rynas) and accepts the user rynas only.
- rynas-web.service: the web interface and HTTP API, unprivileged (user
  rynas), on HTTPS port 8443 (and plain HTTP 8080 during the transition).
  Options in /etc/default/rynas-web.

Both are enabled and started on installation. Binaries live in
/usr/libexec/rynas: they are daemons, not commands for users. rynasctl, also
there, is the root helper of the units generated for scheduled tasks
(rynas-task-<id>.service/.timer) and of the apt hook of system restore points
(/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/80rynas-snapshot); both are written by rynas when the
corresponding settings are applied, not shipped by the package.

Recommended packages
--------------------
Each feature runs the system tools: samba, nfs-kernel-server, acl,
avahi-daemon and wsdd2 (file services and discovery), btrbk and btrfs-progs
(snapshots), mdadm, cryptsetup-bin, systemd-cryptsetup, gdisk, smartmontools
and hdparm (storage). Without one of them only its feature fails, with a
message naming the missing tool.

Deduplication engine (rynas-oans)
---------------------------------
Deduplication of shared folders uses oans, packaged as rynas-oans (binary in
/usr/lib/rynas/oans, outside PATH). Storage > volume > Deduplication offers
to install it: from an apt source that has it, otherwise from the .deb
attached to the rynas release, copied to /var/lib/rynas/packages/ (rynasd
installs it only when its SHA-256 is one rynas knows). btrfs-compsize is
recommended with it.

NUT newer than Debian's (rynas NUT packages)
--------------------------------------------
Install NUT (Hardware > UPS) installs Debian's nut. When UPS setup finds a UPS
that needs a newer NUT than the system's (USB 0925:1234 needs NUT 2.8.5; Debian
13 has 2.8.1), it offers Update NUT: from an apt source with a new enough NUT,
then from a trixie-backports source already configured (System > Updates has a
switch for it), otherwise from rynas's rebuild of Debian's NUT for Debian 13
(packages nut-server, nut-client, libupsclient7 and libnutscan4, version
2.8.5-0~rynas13+1, attached to the release nut-2.8.5-0rynas1): rynasd uses
them from /var/lib/rynas/packages/ when they are there, otherwise downloads
them from that release, and installs them only when every SHA-256 is one rynas
knows. Any Debian build of NUT 2.8.5 (backports included) replaces them.

Backup of the configuration
---------------------------
    /usr/libexec/rynas/rynasd export --out nas.rynasbak --passphrase-file pass.txt
    /usr/libexec/rynas/rynasd import nas.rynasbak --passphrase-file pass.txt \
        --owner alice [--restore-secrets] [--restore-notifications]

The archive holds the configuration (scheduled tasks and snapshot jobs
included) and the notification settings; with a passphrase also the TOTP
secrets and the key of the notification passwords. The run history of
scheduled tasks is not included. Import stages the configuration for review
in the web interface; --restore-notifications needs rynasd stopped.

First login
-----------
Open https://<nas-address>:8443/ and log in with a system account (PAM) that
is a member of the group rynas-admin or sudo. The package does not add
anyone to these groups. For example:

    adduser alice rynas-admin

Group membership is read at every login, no restart is needed.

HTTPS
-----
rynas-web serves HTTPS on port 8443, IPv4 and IPv6 ([::]:8443,
RYNAS_WEB_TLS_LISTEN in /etc/default/rynas-web). This port never goes through
a reverse proxy: it is the way in that keeps working when everything else
is misconfigured (like DSM's 5001 or Proxmox's 8006).

The certificate:
- On its first start rynasd creates a local CA for this NAS (ECDSA P-256,
  10 years) and a certificate from it (397 days) for every name the NAS
  answers to: the host name, <host>.local, localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1 and the
  addresses of its interfaces (not container bridges, not fe80:: link-local
  IPv6, not temporary IPv6 privacy addresses). It is re-issued by itself when
  the host name or an address changes (checked every 2 minutes) and 30 days
  before it expires; rynas-web loads the new one within 10 seconds, without a
  restart and without dropping open pages.
- Browsers warn about it until you trust the local CA: download it with
  GET /api/v1/system/tls/ca (a page in the web interface follows) or copy
  /etc/rynas/tls/ca.crt, and add it to the system or browser trust store.
  Trusting the CA once keeps working when the certificate is re-issued.
  Compare its SHA-256 fingerprint (GET /api/v1/system/tls) before trusting
  it.
- Your own certificate (from your CA or a public one) is imported with
  POST /api/v1/system/tls/import (a page in the web interface follows): the
  full chain (server certificate first) and its unencrypted key. rynas
  checks that the key belongs to the certificate, that each certificate
  signs the previous one, and that it is valid now, and warns when it
  expires within 30 days or does not cover this NAS's names. An imported
  certificate is never renewed by rynas.
- Files: rynasd keeps the CA key and every certificate in /var/lib/rynas/tls
  (0700 root) and publishes the active one for rynas-web in /etc/rynas/tls:
  ui.crt (chain), ui.key (0640 root:rynas), ca.crt. Do not edit them by hand:
  rynasd rewrites them. "systemctl reload rynas-web" re-reads them at once.
- Locked out by an imported certificate your browser refuses? As root:
      /usr/libexec/rynas/rynasd tls-reset
  makes rynas's own certificate active again (rynas-web picks it up by
  itself). From this host, http://127.0.0.1:8080/ also keeps working (an SSH
  tunnel: ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080 <nas>).
- No HSTS is sent on port 8443: with a certificate the browser does not
  trust yet, HSTS would remove the button that lets you continue.

Transition from plain HTTP (port 8080):
- Port 8080 still answers. Once the certificate is loaded, requests from
  other hosts get a temporary redirect (307, not cached by browsers) to
  https://<same host>:8443/ with the same path. Requests from this host
  (127.0.0.1, ::1) are never redirected: a reverse proxy on the NAS and SSH
  tunnels keep working.
- --no-https-redirect (RYNAS_WEB_OPTS) serves plain HTTP to everyone, as
  before; RYNAS_WEB_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8080 closes plain HTTP to the network.
- The session cookie is marked Secure over HTTPS, so a session started on
  8443 is never sent over plain HTTP. Log in again after switching ports.
- A reverse proxy on another host that talks to 8080 and terminates TLS
  itself: set RYNAS_WEB_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080 and send X-Forwarded-Proto:
  https (no redirect, Secure cookie), or point it at 8443. Its
  X-Forwarded-For is not trusted: every client counts as the proxy's address
  for the login rate limit.

Behind the built-in reverse proxy (rynas-caddy)
-----------------------------------------------
Port 8080 is the upstream of rynas-caddy (80/443). With the default
RYNAS_WEB_LISTEN=auto it moves by itself, without a restart and without
ending sessions:
1. Before rynas-caddy fronts the interface (not installed, external proxy
   mode, not applied yet), 8080 listens on 0.0.0.0 as before.
2. Once rynasd has loaded the interface's route into rynas-caddy it writes
   the names to /run/rynas/web-access.ui. When that file is there and HTTPS
   on 8443 works (listening, certificate loaded), rynas-web moves 8080 to
   127.0.0.1 within 5 seconds. Open the interface at https://<host>.local/
   (rynas-caddy) or https://<nas-address>:8443/ (always).
3. Back to external mode, or rynas-caddy removed: the file goes away and
   8080 listens on 0.0.0.0 again.
With HTTPS off (RYNAS_WEB_TLS_LISTEN=off) or 8443 not working, 8080 never
leaves the network, so there is always a way in.

Upgrading from a release with RYNAS_WEB_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080: an unchanged
/etc/default/rynas-web is replaced by the new one (auto); an edited one is
offered by dpkg, and keeping yours keeps 8080 where you put it. Check with
    grep RYNAS_WEB_LISTEN /etc/default/rynas-web
    ss -ltn 'sport = :8080'
Locked out of 80/443? 8443 does not go through rynas-caddy; from the NAS
itself http://127.0.0.1:8080/ always works (ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080
<nas>).

Client addresses: requests relayed by rynas-caddy reach rynas-web from
127.0.0.1. rynas-web then takes the client's address from the
X-Forwarded-For header Caddy sets (its right-most entry), but only for
connections from loopback to the plain port: never on 8443 and never from
other hosts, where the header is ignored. The login rate limit, the log
line for fail2ban and the lockout notification see the real client.

fail2ban
--------
rynas-web throttles login attempts itself and logs failures as
"authentication failure for USER from IP". To ban persistent guessers in the
firewall too, install fail2ban and copy the examples:

    cp /usr/share/doc/rynas/examples/fail2ban/filter.d/rynas.conf /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/
    cp /usr/share/doc/rynas/examples/fail2ban/jail.d/rynas.conf /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/
    systemctl reload fail2ban

Removal
-------
"apt remove rynas" stops the services and keeps configuration and state.
"apt purge rynas" also deletes /var/lib/rynas (configuration database, TOTP
secrets, notification settings, task history, certificates and the local CA),
/etc/rynas/tls, the scheduled task units, the
btrbk configurations in /etc/rynas (Debian's own nightly btrbk.service is skipped by a drop-in until /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf exists), the apt hook, and the user and groups
rynas and rynas-admin. Shares, users and their files are left in place.
